1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,950
A forklift passes a worker.

2
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:04,350
A crowd presses toward a gate.

3
00:00:04,400 --> 00:00:05,950
A queue backs up.

4
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:08,408
The cameras saw it all.

5
00:00:08,458 --> 00:00:09,883
Nobody was watching.

6
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:11,800
This is Rulecam.

7
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:15,450
A warehouse manager sets one rule, in Spanish:

8
00:00:15,500 --> 00:00:18,950
keep people four metres from a moving forklift.

9
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:20,664
Muse Spark plans it.

10
00:00:20,714 --> 00:00:25,379
SAM 3.1 finds the forklift and measures the floor in metres.

11
00:00:25,429 --> 00:00:27,950
What it can't see, it says.

12
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:31,450
Before anyone approves, Rule CI attacks the rule.

13
00:00:31,500 --> 00:00:34,950
Sixteen edge cases, six broken copies. All caught.

14
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:36,689
Now it fires twice.

15
00:00:36,739 --> 00:00:39,298
A worker walks inside four metres.

16
00:00:39,348 --> 00:00:44,950
Muse Glimmer, Meta's open model on our own GPU, checks the frames: violation.

17
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:47,750
Then the rule flags the driver. Glimmer:

18
00:00:47,800 --> 00:00:50,950
that's the operator, in the cab. No alert.

19
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:54,050
The warning goes out in Spanish.

20
00:00:55,000 --> 00:00:58,428
In a crowd crush, nobody does anything wrong.

21
00:00:58,478 --> 00:01:01,472
People are packed too tightly to move.

22
00:01:01,522 --> 00:01:04,950
From the ground, no one sees it building.

23
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:11,950
This is real footage from a crowd experiment, played live into Rulecam, with an instruction in Hindi.

24
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:14,912
SAM 3.1 counts heads in each zone:

25
00:01:14,962 --> 00:01:18,296
ninety-five to ninety-nine percent of the true count.

26
00:01:18,346 --> 00:01:22,950
At the gate, eight people per square metre. The truth: eight.

27
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:31,883
Muse Spark calls it critical, tells stewards to stop the inflow, and writes a calm announcement in Hindi and English.

28
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:34,521
Reading to alert: about forty seconds.

29
00:01:34,571 --> 00:01:37,950
A slow check never holds a warning back.

30
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:40,617
A shop owner doesn't want rules.

31
00:01:40,667 --> 00:01:41,950
They want answers.

32
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:44,583
Muse Spark picks its own tools:

33
00:01:44,633 --> 00:01:48,972
SQL over SAM's tracks, SAM counts, Glimmer looking at frames.

34
00:01:49,022 --> 00:01:53,800
It splits the view into two lanes by itself, and answers.

35
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:59,950
On shelves, Spark spots the gaps and SAM checks no product is there.

36
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:06,950
It reads local food recalls, and keeps only items it can link to a real page.

37
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:11,300
And one rule pack can run in every store.

38
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:14,093
Why trust any of this?

39
00:02:14,143 --> 00:02:16,664
You don't have to trust us.

40
00:02:16,714 --> 00:02:23,950
Every event, verdict and action goes into a hash chain, and your own browser recomputes every fingerprint.

41
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:27,050
Change one byte, and it breaks.

42
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:31,077
Workers see every rule on a poster.

43
00:02:31,127 --> 00:02:36,883
One tap disputes an alert, and a false alarm becomes a permanent test.

44
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:39,217
No face recognition. Ever.

45
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:43,067
Need a rule type we don't have?

46
00:02:43,117 --> 00:02:49,300
Muse Code writes it, and a solver proves it never alerts without its target.

47
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:52,690
Rulecam. Plain words in. Verified alerts out.

48
00:02:52,740 --> 00:02:56,800
On the cameras you already own, in any language.
